  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/c8cbd5b93633e4e4899a1cde0da618f3be792bdbbd77db650c32814187b222f8.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/6708537b79154a7778fe52e8d82b6577f93d40a06d3af0267e3e4b7eaa6468a9.png)\n\nIn $\u25b3OMR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OM=3|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ and $q$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=-3q.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=2|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=2p.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =-3q+2p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=2p-3q.$\n\nSince $p\u2226q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$",
